/T (Input receipt. The server receives the chip UID, the payload string P, and the base64url signature from the consumer's device over an HTTPS POST request. Signature decoding. The signature is base64url-decoded and DER-decoded to recover the integer pair \(r, s\). Values are validated to ensure r and s both fall in the range [1, n-1]. Out-of-range values immediately indicate a forged or malformed signature. Hash recomputation. The server recomputes H' = SHA-256\(P\) from the received payload string. Verification computation. The server computes: w = s-1 mod n; u1 = H' times w mod n; u2 = r times w mod n; then \(x1, y1\) = u1 times G plus u2 times Q, where Q is the ECDSA public key. The signature is valid if and only if x1 mod n equals r. Database cross-reference. If the signature is valid, the server confirms that the UID is registered, the product record is active, and the tag has not been flagged by the fraud detection system.)
/E (Input receipt. The server receives the chip UID, the payload string P, and the base64url signature from the consumer's device over an HTTPS POST request. Signature decoding. The signature is base64url-decoded and DER-decoded to recover the integer pair \(r, s\). Values are validated to ensure r and s both fall in the range [1, n-1]. Out-of-range values immediately indicate a forged or malformed signature. Hash recomputation. The server recomputes H' = SHA-256\(P\) from the received payload string. Verification computation. The server computes: w = s-1 mod n; u1 = H' times w mod n; u2 = r times w mod n; then \(x1, y1\) = u1 times G plus u2 times Q, where Q is the ECDSA public key. The signature is valid if and only if x1 mod n equals r. Database cross-reference. If the signature is valid, the server confirms that the UID is registered, the product record is active, and the tag has not been flagged by the fraud detection system.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [19 21 20 22]
>>
endobj
